21:46:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Religious texts can matter, especially when political movements selectively interpret them to justify violence. It would be too simple to say ideology plays no role. But texts alone do not explain why only a tiny fraction of people who read or inherit the same texts become violent. If the words themselves were the sufficient “root cause,” you would expect violence to be common and consistent among Muslims across countries and generations. It is not. A more accurate view is: - **Texts contain passages that can be read in harsh or militant ways**, as do many old religious and political traditions. - **Interpretation matters:** mainstream Muslim scholarship generally reads warfare-related passages in historical, legal, and ethical contexts, not as blanket permission to attack civilians. - **Extremist groups choose particular interpretations** and pair them with political goals: overthrowing governments, sectarian domination, resistance to occupation, or building a state. - **Conditions matter because they determine whether extremist interpretations gain traction.** War, repression, state collapse, prison radicalization, social networks, propaganda, and grievances can make recruitment and mobilization far more likely. Those are not excuses for violence; they help explain its causes and therefore how to prevent it. Calling political and social factors “excuses” confuses explanation with justification. You can condemn an attacker completely while still asking why some environments produce armed networks and others do not. Security policy has to understand those mechanisms or it will miss the actual warning signs. A fair formulation would be: > “Some extremist interpretations of Islamic texts are genuinely dangerous and should be criticized. But it does not follow that the texts make Muslims generally dangerous. Violence emerges when an extremist ideology is adopted and organized under particular conditions—not from ordinary Muslim identity alone.”
