21:49:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m not saying this is the only important threat, or that concerns about terrorism should be ignored. Violent extremist groups, wars, state repression, organized crime, climate risks, public-health threats, and many other problems all deserve attention. I’m focusing on this because it is the question you raised, and because the way we define a threat affects whether we respond effectively. If the concern is **violent Islamist extremism**, then the useful focus is on extremist networks, recruitment, propaganda, financing, conflict zones, credible threats, and attack planning. That is an actual security problem. If the concern becomes **Muslims as a whole**, then attention shifts toward a huge population of mostly ordinary people who are not implicated by any of those indicators. That is both unfair and a poor use of caution: it can distract from people and groups showing genuine warning signs, whatever ideology they claim. So the point is not to make your opinion the world’s biggest issue. It is to keep “real threats” and “broad identity-based suspicion” from being treated as the same thing.
